- PexObserve.ValueForViewing : This is used to provide values for viewing during a test run. These are the values we inspect manually in Visual Studio's IDE while debugging.

- Debug.Assert : This is a way to implement assertions. The .NET framework's Debug.Assert method works by causing a break on the first line if the condition is false. This is useful for finding potential bugs which indicate logical errors in the software.

- PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Contains_x", New_arrListContainsX); : This line is used to display the value of "New_arrListContainsX" for viewing during a test run. "$new_arrList_Contains_x" is a user-friendly name for this variable.

The Pex framework automatically verifies the behavior of the class under test, generating test methods for every public method decorated with PexMethod, taking each parameter individually in a loop, and running mutation testing. It even supports data access and modification for parameters, and can generate random data for method calls.

After the run is completed, the Pex framework can generate a list of potential issues found during testing. It can also generate a detailed report which shows a lot of information, including which conditions led to the failure, the expected and the actual results, which can help us to validate the results and to make sure that our analysis is correct.

To add, NUnit is a simple framework to write unit tests in C# or any other .NET language. It supports a wide range of features, like test setup and cleanup, multiple assertions, timeouts, etc. It also has a rich set of attributes you can use to control how your tests are structured and run.

The Pex tool combined with the NUnit framework provides a very comprehensive set of tools for unit testing, where the framework generates a set of tests for a class, runs them, and provides a detailed report if anything goes wrong, highlighting the specific conditions leading to failures and the results of individual tests.

Remember that these are just basic use cases. The framework is capable of much more, depending on your specific needs and scenarios.

To validate a complex class, let's say in the case of a BankAccount class, a test method for each method of this class needs to be written. And for complex data types like a BankAccount, NUnit offers a rich set of assertions to verify the values. In this class, methods to deposit and withdraw money from bank accounts should be tested.

Note that testing a complex class typically involves many different aspects, involving not only testing the individual methods, but testing all possible path of execution, including boundary values, illegal values, and possible exceptions may be thrown. The earlier test case is testing a simple data class, just testing whether the object created successfully. But in complex case, we need to validate each method individually in multiple conditions. It's a way to ensure the functionality of your code works as expected. Also, it helps to identify if any part of the code is not handling the exceptions properly.

The test cases include method parameters and expected return values. When a test case (method call) is run, the method is executed with the parameters given in the test case, and the returned value is checked against the expected value provided in the test case. If the returned value matches the expected value, the test passes. If it does not match, the test fails and provides a detailed message explaining why the test failed. The NUnit framework and Pex can help to generate these tests for you, allowing you to focus on other aspects of development, as the framework takes care of checking each method call with its own set of parameters. - 출처 : http://www.itworld.co.kr/news/115122#cs-013-01-02-02 

Unit testing, through the NUnit framework and the P--------------------        }
